## Key-Value Storage with Map

`Map[K, V]` stores key-value pairs. Look up values by key in O(1) average time. Keys must implement `Hash` and `Eq`.

### Creating and Using Map

```xiom
let scores = Map[Str, Int]();
scores.insert("Alice", 95);
scores.insert("Bob", 87);
scores.insert("Carol", 92);

let alice_score = scores.get(&"Alice");  // Some(95)
let dave_score = scores.get(&"Dave");    // None
```

### Map Operations

```xiom
let m = Map[Int, Str]();

// Insert
m.insert(1, "one");
m.insert(2, "two");

// Get (returns Option)
let val = m.get(&1);       // Some("one")
let missing = m.get(&99);   // None

// Contains
let has_one = m.contains(&1);    // true
let has_nine = m.contains(&9);   // false
```

### Updating Values

```xiom
var m = Map[Str, Int]();
m.insert("count", 0);

// Increment pattern
let current = m.get(&"count").unwrap_or(0);
m.insert("count", current + 1);
```

### Bulk Insert

```xiom
fn build_count(words: &Vec[Str]) -> Map[Str, Int] {
  let counts = Map[Str, Int]();
  var i = 0;
  while i < words.len() {
    let key = words[i];
    let current = counts.get(&key).unwrap_or(0);
    counts.insert(key, current + 1);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return counts;
}
```

### Key Requirements

Keys must implement `Hash` and `Eq`. Built-in types (Int, Str, Bool) implement these automatically. Custom types need `derive[Hash, Eq]`.

